  <p>
    Melasma is symmetrical brown or greyish-brown patching, usually across the cheeks, forehead,
    upper lip and bridge of the nose. It is more common in women, more common in Indian and other
    deeper skin tones, and it is driven by a combination of sun exposure, visible light, heat and
    hormonal influence in skin that is genetically prone to it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The single most useful thing to understand at the start:
    <strong>this is a condition that is controlled, not cured.</strong> Treatment can lighten it
    very substantially. It will come back without maintenance and sun protection. A plan sold on
    the promise of permanent removal will disappoint.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes it worse</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Sun exposure, including on cloudy days and through car and office windows</li>
    <li>Visible light, which ordinary clear sunscreens do not block</li>
    <li>Heat — cooking over a flame, long sun exposure, hot work environments</li>
    <li>Pregnancy, hormonal contraception and hormone treatment</li>
    <li>Thyroid disease in some patients</li>
    <li>Irritation from scrubs, strong peels or over-aggressive treatment</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    Examination establishes the pattern and distribution, and dermoscopy helps judge whether the
    pigment sits superficially, deeper, or both — which changes how well it will respond and how
    cautious treatment needs to be. Thyroid function is checked where the history suggests it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Any cosmetic, hair dye or cream in daily use is reviewed, because pigmented contact dermatitis
    looks very similar and will not improve until the trigger is removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <h3>Sun protection — non-negotiable</h3>
  <p>
    A broad-spectrum sunscreen with iron oxide for visible light protection, applied every morning
    and reapplied through the day. Everything else on this list depends on this being done
    properly. Treatment without it is money wasted.
  </p>

  <h3>Topical lightening agents</h3>
  <p>
    Hydroquinone remains the most effective, used in courses rather than continuously. Combination
    creams with a retinoid and a mild steroid work well for a defined period. Non-hydroquinone
    options — azelaic acid, kojic acid, tranexamic acid, cysteamine — are useful for maintenance
    and for patients who cannot use hydroquinone.
  </p>

  <h3>Oral tranexamic acid</h3>
  <p>
    Helpful in resistant melasma, with a clear caution: it is not suitable for anyone with a
    history of clots, and it needs assessment before it is prescribed.
  </p>

  <h3>Peels and lasers</h3>
  <p>
    Gentle peels help as an addition once topical treatment is established. Laser toning has a
    place in selected patients at conservative settings. Both can worsen melasma if used too
    aggressively or too early, which is why they come after topical treatment rather than instead
    of it.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Why melasma so often gets worse with treatment</h3>
    <p>
      Almost always one of three things: aggressive laser or peel settings, a fairness cream
      containing a steroid, or stopping sunscreen once it started improving. Slow and steady, with
      sun protection held throughout, beats anything fast.
    </p>
  </div>
